<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Kubrat</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. c. 665</span> · <a href="/centuries/7th-century">7th century</a> · Also known as Khan Kubrat · Of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dulo-clan">Dulo clan</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Kubrat was a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bulgars">Bulgar</a> ruler of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dulo-clan">Dulo clan</a> who, in the 630s, threw off <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/avars">Avar</a> or Turkic overlordship and united the Onogur, Bulgar and related tribes of the steppe north of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/black-sea">Black Sea</a> into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/old-great-bulgaria">Old Great Bulgaria</a>. Raised partly at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a> and baptised there, he was an ally of Emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/heraclius">Heraclius</a>. After his death his sons divided the people, and one, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asparuh-of-bulgaria">Asparukh</a>, founded the Bulgarian state on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/danube">Danube</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/kubrat.jpg" alt="Kubrat" width="189" height="435" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Dimitar Atanasov Gyudjenov (1891 - 1979) · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Giudjenov_Kubrat_and_his_sons_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The revolt of Samo's Slavs around 623 and the rise of <b>Kubrat</b>'s Great Bulgaria in the 630s were both reactions against Avar lordship, and so were linked to the formation of new states.</blockquote><a href="/empire/avar-khaganate">Avar Khaganate · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>The Bulgars were a Turkic-speaking steppe people who in the early 7th century formed the confederation known as Old Great Bulgaria under <b>Khan Kubrat</b>, north of the Black Sea.</blockquote><a href="/empire/first-bulgarian-empire">First Bulgarian Empire · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>It was established by Turkic-speaking nomadic elites who consolidated power across territories stretching from the lower Volga to the northern Caucasus and the steppes north of the Black Sea, subjugating or displacing rivals such as the Bulgars of <b>Kubrat</b>'s Old Great Bulgaria.</blockquote><a href="/empire/khazar-khaganate">Khazar Khaganate · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Others later joined <b>Kubrat's</b> Old Great Bulgaria.</blockquote><a href="/state/kutrigurs">Kutrigurs</a></li><li><blockquote>In the 630s <b>Kubrat</b> of the Dulo clan threw off the overlordship of the Avars and united the Onogur and Bulgar tribes of the steppe between the Kuban and the lower Dnieper.</blockquote><a href="/state/old-great-bulgaria">Old Great Bulgaria</a></li><li><blockquote>Byzantine writers record their dealings with Constantinople and their conversion attempts; their leader Organa and his nephew <b>Kubrat</b> had ties to the Byzantine court.</blockquote><a href="/state/onogurs">Onogurs</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/phanagoria">Phanagoria</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/russia">Russia</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=45.19,36.83&amp;year=655" aria-label="Phanagoria on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5425 569 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the place of death of Kubrat"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5875 839h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5875 839h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the place of death. <a href="/atlas?at=45.19,36.83&amp;year=655">What was here in 655 CE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
